 Given that Square A have a perimeter of $24$ cm, we can find its side length as follows: \[
\text{Perimeter} = (\text{side length}) \cdot 4 \Rightarrow 24 = 4s_A \Rightarrow s_A = 6 \text{ cm}
\] Find the area of Square A: \[
\text{Area of A} = (\text{side length})^2 \Rightarrow 36 \text{ sq. cm}
\] Since Square B has an area equal to one-fourth of Square A, its area is: \[
\text{Area of B} = \frac{1}{4} \cdot 36 = 9 \text{ sq. cm}
\] Find the side length of Square B: \[
\text{Area of B} = (\text{side length})^2 \Rightarrow 9 = s_B^2 \Rightarrow s_B = 3 \text{ cm}
\] The perimeter of Square B is: \[
\text{Perimeter of B} = (\text{side length}) \cdot 4 \Rightarrow 12 \text{ cm}
\] </think> <answer> $12$ cm

